/-!
# Simultaneous normal forms for outer four-groups

An elementary four-group with inner intersection of order two is generated by
its inner involution and any outer involution. The single involution class in
SL₃ aligns the first generator with diag(1,-1,-1). The second generator is
inner(s) times inverse transpose. Its square forces s to be symmetric, and
projective commutation with the reflection lifts to exact commutation in SL₃.
Thus s has a one-dimensional scalar block and a nondegenerate symmetric binary
block. Finite-field quadratic-form classification takes the binary block to
diag(d,d⁻²), with congruence matrix C satisfying det(C)²=1. The matrix
diag(det(C),Cᵀ) has determinant one, preserves the reflection, and takes s to
diag(d,d,d⁻²). Its literal inner conjugation therefore gives equality with the
standard actor, for every original four-group.

This proves the independent simultaneous normal form used by the fusion step
for Aschbacher--Smith E.1.14(7), printed p.214, via E.1.1--5, pp.209--210,
and the involution conjugacy in A.3.21(a), p.55. The source cites Aschbacher
(1982), Lemma 4.11, for the full generation result. The proof here needs only a
finite field of odd characteristic, together with the supplied simplicity of
PSL₃; the source's prime and congruence restrictions are unnecessary for this
auxiliary statement.

The quadratic-form bridge follows the checked matrix/form conversion in
`Theory/SpecificGroups/PSL3/OddPrimeConjugateBounds/Graph.lean`. The finite-field
classification reuses the attributed development in `SymmetricSimilarity.FiniteForms`;
its ATLAS attribution and Research and Attribution License 1.0 remain in the
imported development and `LICENSES/ATLAS.txt`, `LICENSES/ATLAS-CITATION.cff`.
-/

